<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  A partial Info.plist. GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on, so Xcode merges
	  everything it derives from build settings — bundle id, version, scene
	  manifest, launch screen — on top of what is here.

	  This file exists for one key. Compose Multiplatform refuses to start
	  without it: PlistSanityCheck throws on a background queue a moment after
	  launch, which looks like the app opening and then vanishing.

	  The key opts out of UIKit's 60fps cap on ProMotion displays. Compose
	  drives its own frame pacing, and without this it is limited to half the
	  refresh rate of every recent iPhone — so the check is a refusal to ship
	  something that would feel wrong on the hardware most people have.

	  INFOPLIST_KEY_CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone does not work: that
	  mechanism only passes through keys Xcode knows about, and this is not one.
	-->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  The languages the app ships, so iOS knows it has more than one.

	  Without this the bundle has no .lproj directories — the strings live in
	  Compose Resources, not in the bundle's own localisation folders — so iOS
	  treats the app as English-only and gives it no Language row in Settings.
	  The reader could not change the language anywhere: not in the app, which
	  deliberately does not offer a picker, and not in Settings, which did not
	  know there was a choice.

	  Declaring them is the whole fix. Per-app language then appears in Settings
	  and writes NSLocale.preferredLanguages, which is exactly what Compose
	  Resources reads to pick a `values-*` directory.

	  Keep this in step with the `values-*` folders under composeResources.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>tr</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
